BUILD-DISCIPLINE candidate: base PRs on main; never stack PRs on a shared feature branch.

Observed for real in senechal on 2026-08-05 across a single evening: four PRs (#19, #86, #90, plus #18) were all aimed at one long-lived branch `alert-on-change-not-on-level` instead of at `main`. Every merge into that branch invalidated the siblings still queued behind it, and Zach hit a merge refusal on three separate occasions ("conflicts with ...", "rebase and resolve -- retrying will not help"). Each refusal cost a diagnosis + rebase + re-verify cycle by a subagent.

THE MECHANISM (three ordinary things, vicious in combination):
1. A PR base is a POINTER resolved at merge time, not a snapshot. Move the pointer and every queued PR is now measured against a base it never saw.
2. GitHub squash-merge gives the merged content a NEW sha. Identical content, different identity. Git then reports the same code as "changed in both" and as commits "missing from main" when they are demonstrably present. This wasted real time tonight: `git log origin/main..branch` listed three commits whose content was already on main via squash, and a `git merge-tree` showed lib/common.sh conflicting in a PR that had never touched the conflicting region.
3. Parallel agents all append to the SAME shared files -- in senechal every feature added a helper to `lib/common.sh` and assertions to `health/test-alerting.sh`. So each merge moved the base INSIDE the exact region the next PR edits. Conflicts become arithmetic, not luck.

PROPOSED RULES (for the propagated build-discipline baseline):
- Branch from `origin/main`, always. One target that only moves forward.
- Land small and fast; do not accumulate an integration branch. senechal's reached 1440 insertions across 14 files before it landed.
- When fanning out parallel agents, give each its OWN file where possible (lib/memory.sh, lib/ini.sh sourced by common.sh) rather than having all of them append to one shared file. Disjoint files merge cleanly; a shared append point does not.
- Delete branches after merge. Roughly half of tonight's closeout-lint noise was stale pointers to already-merged work being reported as potential work loss.
- When a base MUST move under live work, prefer `git merge base-branch` into the feature branch over rebase+force-push: a force-push rewrites the base out from under any concurrent agent still working on it. Used deliberately twice tonight for that reason.

WHY REALISATEUR: this is a baseline/ecosystem concern, not a senechal one -- it applies to every repo that fans out subagents, and BUILD-DISCIPLINE.md is where the propagated checklist lives.

Related finding filed separately in spirit: `closeout-lint --strict` asserts a negative over the repo's whole branch inventory rather than the run being gated, so a session that pushed everything it authored still blocks on its neighbours' branches -- including concurrent agents' in-flight branches, where the implied remedy (push it) would publish another run's unfinished work. Same shape as the `silence-audit` BLIND-on-scheduler-domain finding. Three separate senechal subagents independently spent significant effort proving that negative tonight; each concluded `git log <branch> --not --remotes=origin` was empty for every flagged branch. Suggested fix, already drafted and tested against the real repo: skip branches checked out in linked worktrees (the check already declares those BLIND and out of domain one screen earlier, then flags them anyway), and downgrade a branch whose tip is reachable from ANY remote ref from FLAG to a printed note -- reachability, not branch-name existence, is the test for work loss.
